#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "df884ed9-c75e-5a38-abe2-425e01ee4aa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-432" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "317454f0-3a04-5c1c-a451-2b8b2ec850ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-433" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078232 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "189d4a9a-fb2c-57b2-b9f6-9bec14fa1062" :question/math-academy-id "e-434" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73702a1-67af-5e22-b65d-37f5dd1929fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289614} {:answer/id #uuid "942c0acf-063c-5f10-bf3b-6caeeb36c5bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289615} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef29137-7eda-5851-8e87-4646450e55ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289616} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6443d4-e468-5b46-9a8a-cef9c464313e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289617} {:answer/id #uuid "1a34c67c-e9c3-5a6f-b1c0-6fbfca30fdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef29137-7eda-5851-8e87-4646450e55ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289616} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff15138-2413-54b3-b8ab-034b626452de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b6777ca-ff4c-593f-a186-002b5ace07e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117662" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5e053e-2fd0-5b0e-bf97-ca02452a3d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296649} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea249d8-30d5-5804-84dd-69702ba7be1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "62" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296650} {:answer/id #uuid "48686f43-375c-5027-b3aa-9095101538ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296651} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1ea743-cf10-507e-ac95-e3e545c984ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296652} {:answer/id #uuid "84d8ab6b-d244-5fb6-bd0d-7bd83a787197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a1ea743-cf10-507e-ac95-e3e545c984ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296652} :answer-field/id #uuid "826651a7-01f6-5aa1-8623-1d556360809b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bebd9b42-4c15-5a4c-bcc4-3722f4a7881d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117666" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}7-3x, & x<-1 \\\\ 10, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}(7-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(7x-3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{-5}^{-1}+10x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[7(-1)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-7(-5)+3\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}]+10[(1)-(-1)] \\\\  & =[28+36]+10[2] \\\\  & =64+20 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77aaf520-58b8-502a-8c99-aa6ccf2212ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296656} {:answer/id #uuid "89e4c37f-f88c-57b1-9263-b4a9fb440858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296657} {:answer/id #uuid "661af475-d92a-5fa0-a02c-051a8ea83cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296658} {:answer/id #uuid "e921000f-4906-542d-85d7-1f44c483694a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296659} {:answer/id #uuid "366bc398-d197-5005-85ec-47cddefb648e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89e4c37f-f88c-57b1-9263-b4a9fb440858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296657} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeef0fcf-c965-51e2-b18c-4f01c8f232f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eaf2bfbd-fd1d-5fe9-ac11-0df52f6066be" :question/math-academy-id "q-117669" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[(0)-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}-(-2)+\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}]+[(2)^{2}+2-(0)^{2}-(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "682b381d-5e6e-5160-94ad-9d4a8ed756e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296663} {:answer/id #uuid "650bc56e-d4be-56f7-9782-82bca026b5e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296664} {:answer/id #uuid "1adc9afb-e7c0-5fdd-a4d3-c3dd940dc26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296665} {:answer/id #uuid "b683bc48-e786-553b-b5a2-9cff7eb61c5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296666} {:answer/id #uuid "abf7b063-ee50-5728-ad99-832cfe7442cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "650bc56e-d4be-56f7-9782-82bca026b5e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296664} :answer-field/id #uuid "656e0866-f42a-5d57-9e9d-258ff950f4fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e1ef3f5-5fb9-5108-bf98-1048434bd777" :question/math-academy-id "q-117702" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/f725b571954587b7b88426a254d7a6fc05f09b63ab2cc94b81303a510e8c1798.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,3].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f1af6f-a432-58bb-a999-4cd67fd6fa93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296670} {:answer/id #uuid "f95ca29d-d08a-5b85-9f41-f3a7fb4bdd4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296671} {:answer/id #uuid "cc84a1b4-4b51-505e-a8bc-365bdc846107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296672} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f6f531-5914-5ed2-bc2f-e3069da9a038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296673} {:answer/id #uuid "cccf8bd7-143b-5651-84fb-374769cf2501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f6f531-5914-5ed2-bc2f-e3069da9a038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296673} :answer-field/id #uuid "97582334-4c92-5acc-b560-b65db03a5215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3e6f460-32b8-5013-a35b-ba52c5b54bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-18004" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7ba8acc-7bc1-58fe-b4c0-c7b3ab5c8913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7ba8acc-7bc1-58fe-b4c0-c7b3ab5c8913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296677} :answer-field/id #uuid "5621bfe6-26a6-5dc6-8aa5-44d378722e21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3181657-4385-5db5-a4d1-ee05b6d0a850" :question/math-academy-id "q-339328" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/dcc3b282b99d1b74669ea47455f424361258e33497527bd69e29d50c5083aec4.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [-1,4].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=\\pi -4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\pi -4+10 \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a738a47-cd39-59a2-b5ac-361680b66774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a738a47-cd39-59a2-b5ac-361680b66774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296680} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bdd0db3-f8c7-52d3-b149-84b53d46f5de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6bc413dd-9ad2-5c62-b6c1-bf39c8ac810b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339573" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/be0329f2bc3722a6b3381dcfdbed212dc1e647f7f165cefb4c00f939b74b6d74.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can use the additive property of integrals to write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$   The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2}+4(1) \\\\  & =2\\pi +4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi +4+2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi +3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b6eb8b-d7e6-5633-8a80-2e6304a96ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b6eb8b-d7e6-5633-8a80-2e6304a96ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296683} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa20d964-5c3d-55e5-ad89-be71a77cb196" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a300e381-0c39-5f73-b474-493430914749" :question/math-academy-id "q-342008" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+2, & x<0, \\\\ -2x+1, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}(\\frac{1}{2}x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{-4}^{0}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}{0}^{2}+2(0)-(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}+2(-4))]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3-(-{0}^{2}+0)] \\\\  & =[\\,0-(4-8)]\\,+\\,[\\,-9+3-0] \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec980fcb-97ed-5c60-94de-0521d9d7df27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Continuous Function Given the Function Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69c42e2-62a4-585d-9fe1-e50bb4e78dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296686} {:answer/id #uuid "35db7ba8-10f3-5b28-9231-9281b1b81746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35db7ba8-10f3-5b28-9231-9281b1b81746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296687} :answer-field/id #uuid "368004ae-0c60-520c-ae06-2727af4c31ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eac685d5-1f91-5a31-b64f-648b8efa1ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342011" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1, \\\\ -2x+3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{3}(-2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-1}^{1}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3})]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3(3)-(-{1}^{2}+3(1))] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})]\\,+\\,[0-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406edace-aa97-5a5b-8520-10002d54d74f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296690} {:answer/id #uuid "73ea5a8d-7dfa-5d26-b577-81b32d7beaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296691} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bcaa03-156b-5139-8610-76ed1d6d7e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296692} {:answer/id #uuid "96e5fb65-a474-57f3-bdf0-2f33c9f3e495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296693} {:answer/id #uuid "b232b43b-2b81-5bbd-824b-da1e2bb0fd06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6bcaa03-156b-5139-8610-76ed1d6d7e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296692} :answer-field/id #uuid "323901aa-4529-5a40-af1b-221b7a49f6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "348b9583-e8e1-52b7-9364-b3b56e2e0bcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-353385" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/aa21323548eb2147949bac5bb379ed8fa6672d1b9fcb73137fad484c47e3cac9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd8038c8-1046-58e8-84f0-5162159f0c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296697} {:answer/id #uuid "48776417-a438-5f07-b0e6-d31ef8f8314d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296698} {:answer/id #uuid "891c1d62-d5a1-5127-b5aa-b14807a51b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296699} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fb9a25-3870-5d8e-9e54-d2c4d0f5d3fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296700} {:answer/id #uuid "102e1f8a-f770-53ca-91bf-9d06ba760e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48776417-a438-5f07-b0e6-d31ef8f8314d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296698} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a261122-1a15-5bfa-8e2c-cf52c60b75a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97f5c0c0-1b45-5f58-be76-fdc17942bba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-353387" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/877771be920838a330edabe84b07eb817f1f3d7f1343b6866b9c2f078e079a67.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015a1ea7-f5d8-58d7-93cd-6453d6e9c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296704} {:answer/id #uuid "8aee7257-7e21-5a3b-9509-4909fc0ba609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296705} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2a525b-4544-56f6-a730-d5d8ebd065b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296706} {:answer/id #uuid "d942adeb-6f59-5783-a155-0f35a476c34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296707} {:answer/id #uuid "04f30b91-a338-580d-9c54-07e93c40c717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "015a1ea7-f5d8-58d7-93cd-6453d6e9c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296704} :answer-field/id #uuid "b88d8800-3dae-550c-b54f-1cdc36e62d64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e3aa613-866d-5cee-a63d-5bfb35b4f81e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49168" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =ab \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4.5-8 \\\\  & =-3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87eeb99-3e4f-5c43-a2a9-9444183f192b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289669} {:answer/id #uuid "0f693360-1d6e-522c-bf4d-389e29b65467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289670} {:answer/id #uuid "b7336e5b-19d7-54c3-9d8b-d527be2ebcef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289671} {:answer/id #uuid "c93a060d-f9ad-5cad-802e-a562e8258701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289672} {:answer/id #uuid "f207fce9-e9d3-58ed-9d49-136a7c6a537e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7336e5b-19d7-54c3-9d8b-d527be2ebcef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289671} :answer-field/id #uuid "42712ed9-5a3c-5ef9-80d4-fbb4396ae6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54902dd1-a8a7-5a9c-9e30-07d51d056c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-86167" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/bac953cd2389ef36b415dcdce87730f244958ef0fe2f67911878c5cb3bfcafc6.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,6].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,6].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8+4-1 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141d49e8-f32e-5a4a-abcd-24ceeccca4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296711} {:answer/id #uuid "4063a4dc-b823-5fdb-8140-30de1900db1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296712} {:answer/id #uuid "60db4b0c-27a7-5946-909f-9975e6e1c39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296713} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0a2d10-1ef2-582d-beac-9b914952ba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296714} {:answer/id #uuid "e161d8b3-843b-5ecc-8c10-eb58f9ac0244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60db4b0c-27a7-5946-909f-9975e6e1c39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296713} :answer-field/id #uuid "defe8e7a-347e-5635-9919-7bf34825287e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d882b0e9-3bd1-5004-8b40-e8d3ede43086" :question/math-academy-id "q-86168" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/e9e1ba80436256409381b9d1c1762696156abe1cbfb516f03cfb4837ae2330b2.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbdddce-6bb9-5d6f-bd08-be7557a8f000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799} {:answer/id #uuid "4909127d-d3ef-50aa-9e10-a02ce28169eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293800} {:answer/id #uuid "97255af8-a483-51c4-80e3-0129d7413bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293801} {:answer/id #uuid "77f0a833-75df-5102-8814-f868d63b9f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293802} {:answer/id #uuid "cccd6aab-30ef-5d1e-b5c2-16056043112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cccd6aab-30ef-5d1e-b5c2-16056043112d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293803} :answer-field/id #uuid "2014b57f-2aff-53dd-89c9-a9eda5d5e635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f31c4e7-003d-5a6c-9983-dff54f594d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-86174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
